@node Getting Started
@chapter Getting Started
@cindex extra features
@cindex requirements

  Elfeed X adds optional features to Elfeed.  The @samp{x} means
@dfn{extra}.  The package requires Emacs 30.1 or later and Elfeed 4.0.1
or later.  Configure your subscriptions in Elfeed before enabling
scheduled updates.  Tessera is optional; Elfeed X does not depend on
its display adapters or experimental features.

  The features have independent global minor modes.  Loading a library
exposes its settings without starting timers, loading Elfeed, or
activating hooks.  For scheduled updates and continuous reading, put
these forms in your initialization file:

@lisp
(require 'elfeed-x)
(elfeed-x-auto-update-mode 1)
(require 'elfeed-x-search)
(elfeed-x-search-follow-mode 1)
@end lisp

  Then enter Elfeed with @kbd{M-x elfeed}.  The schedule starts after
entry; the first update attempt occurs 15 minutes later.  Open an
article in another window to use continuous reading.  See
@ref{Scheduled Updates} and @ref{Continuous Reading} for their controls.
WebKit support is configured separately (@pxref{WebKit Windows}).

  To disable a feature, call its mode function with @code{-1} or toggle
it with @kbd{M-x}.  From Lisp, a positive argument enables the mode;
zero or a negative argument disables it.  Repeated enabling does not
duplicate hooks or timers.  The modes apply to existing and future
matching buffers.  Use @kbd{M-x customize-group @key{RET} elfeed-x}
to find the package's settings.

@node Scheduled Updates
@chapter Scheduled Updates
@cindex scheduled updates
@cindex overlapping updates

  Elfeed X maintains one repeating timer for updating all subscriptions.
Each @dfn{update attempt} checks Elfeed's retrieval queue and calls
@code{elfeed-update} only when the queue is empty.  Pending and active
requests both prevent another scheduled update.  A busy attempt is
skipped; it does not queue another full update for immediate execution.
The next attempt follows the normal interval.

  Scheduled updates run Elfeed's normal update hooks.  Manual updates
keep their native behavior.  Elfeed X neither cancels retrieval already
in progress nor manages timers created by other configurations.

@deffn Command elfeed-x-auto-update-mode &optional arg
Enable or disable scheduled updates globally.  Enabling starts the timer
after the next @code{elfeed} command.  If a Search, article, or Tree
buffer already exists, enabling starts the timer immediately.  The
first attempt waits for @code{elfeed-x-update-interval} seconds in both
cases.  Closing Elfeed windows does not stop the timer.

Disabling cancels the owned timer and removes automatic startup.
Ongoing retrieval continues.  Repeated enabling preserves an active
timer and its next attempt time.  If the timer was cancelled externally,
enter Elfeed again or enable this mode again to create a new schedule.
@end deffn

@defopt elfeed-x-update-interval
This positive number specifies the interval in seconds between update
attempts and the initial delay.  Its default is @code{900}, or 15
minutes.  Integer and floating-point values are accepted.

Changing the option through Customize or @code{setopt} restarts an
active timer, measuring the new delay from that change.  If no timer is
active, changing the option does not start one.  Non-positive or
non-numeric values are rejected.  Use Customize or @code{setopt} instead
of @code{setq} to update an active schedule.
@end defopt

  For example, use a half-hour interval:

@lisp
(require 'elfeed-x)
(setopt elfeed-x-update-interval 1800)
@end lisp

  An error during a scheduled update is reported by Emacs's timer
machinery.  The repeating timer remains scheduled.  Its next attempt
still checks the native queue.  To display the planned time in another
interface, use the readers described in @ref{Reading the Schedule}.

@node Continuous Reading
@chapter Continuous Reading
@cindex continuous reading
@cindex article windows
@kindex n @r{(Elfeed Search)}
@kindex p @r{(Elfeed Search)}

  Continuous reading updates an already open article window when you
move through Search results.  With the default bindings, @kbd{n}
(@code{next-line}) and @kbd{p} (@code{previous-line}) move point and
display the entry at its new position.  Focus stays in Search.  Merely
moving through Search never opens an article window.

@deffn Command elfeed-x-search-follow-mode &optional arg
Enable or disable continuous reading in all Search buffers.  After a
configured navigation command, display the entry at point in the first
non-dedicated article window in cyclic window order after the selected
window, on the selected frame.  Windows on other frames are not used.

Mark the displayed entry as read, preserving Search point and the
selected window.  Empty rows and an entry already displayed in that
window do not cause rendering.  Disabling removes the navigation hooks
from existing and future Search buffers.
@end deffn

  Elfeed X identifies article buffers by their major mode, so renamed
buffers and @code{elfeed-show-unique-buffers} are supported.  It uses
Elfeed's article renderer even if a feed has an external
@code{:show-entry} handler; navigation does not launch that handler.
This affects automatic following only.  Explicit native display commands
retain their usual behavior.

@defopt elfeed-x-search-follow-commands
This list contains command symbols that trigger following after they
run.  The default is @code{(next-line previous-line)}.  Recognition uses
the executed command, not the last typed character, so rebinding those
commands preserves following.  Add a custom navigation command to this
list if it should also trigger following.  Changes take effect on the
next command.
@end defopt

  Choose your window layout with Elfeed's
@code{elfeed-show-entry-switch}.  For example, this user configuration
opens articles below Search and retains focus there:

@lisp
(with-eval-after-load 'elfeed-show
  (setopt elfeed-show-entry-switch
          (lambda (buffer)
            (display-buffer
             buffer
             '((display-buffer-reuse-window
                display-buffer-below-selected))))))
@end lisp

  Open an article explicitly to establish that layout.  Subsequent
following reuses the existing article window without calling the
configured window switch function.  It does not change your global
@code{elfeed-search-remain-on-entry} or window settings.

@node WebKit Windows
@chapter WebKit Windows
@cindex WebKit
@cindex widget dimensions

  The optional @code{elfeed-webkit} package renders articles with an
embedded browser.  Its renderer can initially size a widget using the
selected Search window.  When the article occupies a different window,
Elfeed X can fit the widget to the article's actual window after
rendering, without moving focus.

@deffn Command elfeed-x-webkit-mode &optional arg
Enable or disable article widget fitting globally.  Enabling installs
an @code{elfeed-show-update-hook} function and fits existing visible
article widgets.  After rendering, fit the current article's widget to
a window displaying its buffer, preferring the selected frame and then
other visible frames.  Hidden articles and buffers without widgets are
ignored.  If a buffer appears in several windows, one window determines
its widget dimensions.

This mode does not enable a renderer or install key bindings.  Without
xwidget support it has no effect.  Disabling removes the hook; it does
not switch renderers or undo the last widget size.  Fitting occurs after
rendering, not automatically after every window size change.
@end deffn

  Install @code{elfeed-webkit} separately and use an Emacs built with
xwidget support.  The following user configuration enables WebKit when
available and binds @kbd{%} to @code{elfeed-webkit-toggle} in article
buffers:

@lisp
(with-eval-after-load 'elfeed-show
  (when (and (featurep 'xwidget-internal)
             (require 'elfeed-webkit nil t))
    (elfeed-webkit-enable)
    (keymap-set elfeed-show-mode-map "%" #'elfeed-webkit-toggle)
    (require 'elfeed-x-webkit)
    (elfeed-x-webkit-mode 1)))
@end lisp

  The upstream toggle changes Elfeed's global renderer selection; it is
not limited to the current article.  Elfeed's ordinary renderer remains
available through that toggle.  Without xwidget support or the optional
package, this example leaves ordinary rendering in place.

@node Reading the Schedule
@chapter Reading the Schedule
@cindex next update time
@cindex timer readers

  Programs can inspect Elfeed X's schedule without changing it.  These
functions require only @code{elfeed-x}; they do not load Elfeed or start
a timer.  They report this package's timer, not arbitrary timers from
other configurations.

@defun elfeed-x-update-timer
Return the active timer object owned by Elfeed X, or @code{nil} when no
such timer is registered.  A cancelled timer is reported as @code{nil}.
Treat the returned object as read-only; use
@code{elfeed-x-auto-update-mode} to control its lifecycle.
@end defun

@defun elfeed-x-next-update-time
Return the timer's next scheduled time as an Emacs time value, or
@code{nil} without an active timer.  This is the next planned attempt,
not a guarantee of retrieval: the attempt may be delayed by a busy
Emacs or skipped because Elfeed is already fetching feeds.
@end defun

  For example, when using Tessera's Elfeed header, supply a function
that accepts its rendering context and returns the scheduled time:

@lisp
(require 'elfeed-x)

(defun my-elfeed-next-update-time (_context)
  "Return the next planned Elfeed X update time."
  (elfeed-x-next-update-time))

(with-eval-after-load 'tessera-elfeed-search
  (setopt tessera-elfeed-search-header-line-next-update-function
          #'my-elfeed-next-update-time))
@end lisp

  This integration is optional.  For the header provider contract,
see @ref{Header Providers,,, tessera, Tessera}.

